<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Demchugdongrub</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1902">1902</a>–<a href="/years/1966">1966</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Prince De, De Wang</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Demchugdongrub, known as Prince De (De Wang), was an Inner Mongolian prince who led a movement for Mongol autonomy from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> in the 1930s. Turning to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> for support, he headed the Mongol Military Government from 1936 and the collaborationist Mengjiang government at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhangjiakou">Kalgan</a>. After 1945 he fled to the Mongolian People's Republic, was handed to China and was imprisoned until 1963.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/demchugdongrub.jpg" alt="Demchugdongrub" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Roman Fajans · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Prince_Demchugdongrub_NAC_1930s.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
